# QTT Environment Configuration
# Copy this file to .env and uncomment what you need. `make run`, `make postgres_run` and `make mssql_run` load .env
# from the repository root; for `make dev`, load it into your shell first: set -a; . ./.env; set +a; make dev
# NEVER commit .env to version control (it is in .gitignore).
# All settings: docs/configuration.md

# =============================================================================
# Password Encryption (recommended for production)
# =============================================================================
# Encrypts datasource passwords in the database (AES-256-GCM). Off unless enabled here; when off, passwords are
# stored in plain text. Passwords already stored in plain text are encrypted at startup once it is on.
# Keep the key and salt safe: changing either makes stored passwords unreadable (see docs/configuration.md for
# rotation).
#PASSWORD_ENCRYPTION_ENABLED=true
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
#PASSWORD_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 16
#PASSWORD_ENCRYPTION_SALT=

# =============================================================================
# Database (optional - defaults to embedded Derby)
# =============================================================================
# The database type is detected from DB_URL. `make postgres_run` and `make mssql_run` set these for their local
# containers; values here override them.
# Derby:      jdbc:derby:data/database;create=true
# PostgreSQL: jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/qtt
# SQL Server: jdbc:sqlserver://localhost:1433;databaseName=qtt;encrypt=true;trustServerCertificate=true
#DB_URL=jdbc:derby:data/database;create=true
#DB_USER=user
#DB_PASSWORD=password

# =============================================================================
# Redis (optional - shared query result cache)
# =============================================================================
# `make start_redis` runs a local Redis on localhost:6379.
#REDIS_ENABLED=true
#REDIS_HOST=localhost
#REDIS_PORT=6379
#REDIS_PASSWORD=
# Request coalescing prevents duplicate backend calls when multiple requests
# arrive for the same cache key while the first is still fetching from the backend
#CACHE_COALESCING_ENABLED=true
#CACHE_COALESCING_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# =============================================================================
# SPARQi AI assistant (optional)
# =============================================================================
# Any OpenAI-compatible chat API works. Base URL examples:
#   OpenAI:        https://api.openai.com/v1
#   LiteLLM proxy: http://localhost:4000
#   Azure OpenAI:  https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/deployments/{deployment}
#   Ollama:        http://localhost:11434/v1
#SPARQI_ENABLED=true
#SPARQI_LLM_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
#SPARQI_LLM_API_KEY=
#SPARQI_LLM_MODEL=gpt-4o
# Defaults shown:
#SPARQI_LLM_TIMEOUT=90
#SPARQI_LLM_TEMPERATURE=0.7
#SPARQI_LLM_MAX_TOKENS=4000
#SPARQI_SESSION_TIMEOUT=60

# =============================================================================
# Security Notes
# =============================================================================
# 1. Generate strong random values for PASSWORD_ENCRYPTION_KEY and PASSWORD_ENCRYPTION_SALT
# 2. Use different keys for each environment (dev, staging, production)
# 3. Store production secrets in secure secrets management (Kubernetes Secrets, AWS Secrets Manager, etc.)
# 4. Treat SPARQI_LLM_API_KEY like any other secret
